@section Ownership, moves, and borrowing

Scheme has a garbage collector: a value stays alive as long as something can
reach it, and the language never asks who is responsible for it. Rust has no
garbage collector, and instead gives every value exactly one
@newterm{owner} at a time, decided at compile time. When the owner goes out
of scope, the value is dropped; when the owner is a binding that gets
assigned to another binding, the value @newterm{moves} and the old binding
can no longer be used.

@example rust
let words = vec![String::from("borrow"), String::from("checker")];
let moved = words;
println!("@{@}", moved.len());
// => 2
@end example

After the second line, @code{words} has moved into @code{moved}; using
@code{words} again is a compile error, not a stale reference or a use-after-
free. This is the ownership half of the story the later chapters keep coming
back to: chapter 3's environment model (@ref{0.3}, re-cut around this
mechanism) explains sharing in terms of who owns what, and @ref{0.7}
introduces the escape hatch (@code{Rc}) for values that genuinely need more
than one owner.

Moving everything, always, would make functions unpleasant to call: passing a
@code{Vec} to a function that only needs to read it would give that value
away for good. @newterm{Borrowing} lends access without transferring
ownership. A shared borrow, @code{&T}, lets any number of readers look at a
value at once; a mutable borrow, @code{&mut T}, lets exactly one writer
change it, and the compiler never allows a mutable borrow to coexist with any
other borrow of the same value.

@example rust
fn total_len(words: &[String]) -> usize @{
    words.iter().map(String::len).sum()
@}

let words = vec![String::from("borrow"), String::from("checker")];
let len = total_len(&words);
println!("@{len@}");
// => 13
println!("@{@}", words.len());
// => 2
@end example

@code{total_len} takes @code{&[String]}, a borrowed slice, so the caller
keeps @code{words} afterward. A mutable borrow works the same way but grants
exclusive access:

@example rust
fn append(words: &mut Vec<String>, word: impl Into<String>) @{
    words.push(word.into());
@}

let mut words = words;
append(&mut words, "words");
println!("@{words:?@}");
// => ["borrow", "checker", "words"]
@end example

While @code{append}'s @code{&mut words} borrow is alive, no other borrow of
@code{words}, shared or mutable, may coexist with it; the compiler enforces
this at every call site, not only inside @code{append} itself. The runnable
listing combining all three examples above,
@code{examples/sec_0_3_ownership.rs}, also contrasts borrowing with a fourth
function that takes @code{Vec<String>} by value and consumes it outright,
the way none of the three above do.

@newterm{The borrow checker} is the part of the compiler that enforces these
rules; refusing to compile a program that could conceivably violate them is
not a false alarm, it is the point. Five short cases below fix the rules this
book relies on afterward; each is also a doctest on
@code{crates/ch00/src/sec_0_3.rs}, so the verdicts are the compiler's, not an
author's claim.

@table @asis
@item Case A: a move, then a read of the moved-from binding
@example rust
let a = String::from("case a");
let b = a;
println!("@{a@} @{b@}");
@end example
Rejected: @code{a} moved into @code{b} on the previous line.

@item Case B: a shared borrow, read, then a mutation
@example rust
let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];
let first = &v[0];
println!("@{first@}");
v.push(4);
@end example
Accepted: @code{first}'s last use is the @code{println!}, so its borrow has
already ended by the time @code{push} needs @code{v} mutably. This is
non-lexical borrow checking: a borrow's scope is where it is last used, not
the enclosing block.

@item Case C: the same shared borrow, read after the mutation
@example rust
let mut v = vec![1, 2, 3];
let first = &v[0];
v.push(4);
println!("@{first@}");
@end example
Rejected: now @code{first} has to stay valid across the @code{push}, which
needs @code{v} mutably while @code{first} still borrows it immutably.

@item Case D: a reference into data the function is about to drop
@example rust
fn shout() -> &str @{
    let s = String::from("hi");
    &s
@}
@end example
Rejected, and not because of a lifetime annotation you forgot to write:
@code{s} is a local that this function drops on return, and no lifetime
could make a reference to it outlive the function.

@item Case E: clone instead of move
@example rust
let a = String::from("case e");
let b = a.clone();
println!("@{a@} @{b@}");
@end example
Accepted: @code{clone} makes an independent copy, so both bindings stay
valid. Cloning has a real cost (an allocation and a copy here); reach for it
when you genuinely need two independent values, not as a reflex for
appeasing the compiler.
@end table

